<div class="summary">
    <p>
        Undergraduate research assistant on a capstone team led by PhD student Satvik Checkuri under Dr. Edward
        Fox, working on information retrieval over a database of Electronic Theses and Dissertations. I kept the
        Linux VMs running, wrote the Python automation that cut a manual filtering step from about two months to
        two weeks, and labeled several hundred abstracts by section. It was the team’s project rather than mine;
        I owned specific pieces of it.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>How it started</h2>
<p>
    In the early months of summer 2024 I was emailing anyone at Virginia Tech who might take an undergraduate
    for research. Out of a lot of emails I got one lead: pilot-testing a study that was about to launch. The
    study was short, but it introduced me to Dr. Edward Fox, who later offered me a volunteer assistant role in
    his research capstone.
</p>

<h2>The team</h2>
<p>
    The capstone was a small group of seniors who were serious about research, led by Satvik Checkuri, a PhD
    student working on information retrieval over Electronic Theses and Dissertations, or ETDs. Fueled by
    graduation and years of programming, they were on a different level from me. I was there to fill out a
    resume. I left wanting to do this for a living.
</p>

<h2>Getting the tools running</h2>
<p>
    My first assignment was the Object Annotator, a tool for identifying sections within an ETD: get it running
    locally, then on a remote VM. Debugging someone else’s codebase on an unfamiliar framework was painful, and
    the README saved me. On the VM, the trick was picking a node whose environment already matched the repo’s
    dependencies so I did not have to change much. It worked. The lesson I took early and have not dropped
    since: documentation matters.
</p>

<h2>The filter</h2>
<p>
    Next was filtering ETDs into a usable training set. Satvik wanted to exclude papers that were too long or
    off-format, and I spent two weeks sifting through more than eight thousand of them by hand to find two
    hundred clean samples. That was enough to push me to automate it.
</p>
<p>
    The script scanned local CSVs for file IDs, pulled the papers from the VM over SSH, ran OCR and regular
    expressions over them to find required fields like department and references, and sorted each one into
    acceptable, needs review, or unacceptable. Two weeks of manual work became about forty-five minutes, and the
    lab’s processing timeline went from around two months to two weeks. Two days of coding for months of saved
    labor.
</p>

<h2>The fall</h2>
<p>
    I stayed on into the fall semester for more debugging and VM setups. This time I failed to get a repository
    running; the semester was heavy, I could not stay on it, and Satvik bailed me out. I also labeled several
    hundred ETD abstracts by section: background, objective, method, result, conclusion.
</p>

<h2>What I took away</h2>
<p>
    I started to enjoy the chaos. I asked Satvik constantly about the PhD path, and he was honest about the
    hours, the grind, and the uncertain payoff, and just as honest about the freedom, the depth, and the
    satisfaction of solving hard problems. I do not know yet whether a PhD is my next step, but this is where I
    started taking the question seriously.
</p>

